      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="780" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, kothari">|FRAME: 1-4-beta-Xylan (1&amp;rarr;4)-&amp;beta;-D-xylan| is present in some trees and other plants in a partially acetylated form. Angiosperm (hardwood) xylans (e.g. from beechwood) have a high rate of substitution (70-80%) by acetyl groups at position 2 and/or 3 of the &amp;beta;-D-xylopyranosyl residues, conferring on the xylan its partial solubility in water |CITS: [8338637]|.</Comment>
